<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 6<br>Part 5 (§§ 51–61 / Oaseyos נא–סא)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:24</span> (<i>Amtza'isa D'Alma</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 51 — "Yaakov Lived in Egypt Seventeen Years": True Life Through Transforming Sorrow to Joy</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yaakov lived in the land of Egypt seventeen years."</span> It is explained in our Sages' words (Midrash Rabbah) and especially in the holy Zohar on this <span class="h">parshah</span> that those seventeen years were the essential years of his life — he lived in joy and contentment, as stated there in the holy Zohar. At first glance this is astonishing: when he dwelt in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> he had no peace, yet in impure Egypt — where the Egyptian exile began and they embittered their lives — specifically there he dwelt in peace!</div>

<div class="p">But all this is the aspect of <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain"</span> etc. The essential completeness of joy in Him (= the essential vitality) is when one overpowers to seize sorrow and sighing and transform it to joy — the essential sorting from the Chambers of Exchange, accomplished through joy, essentially through exchanging and transforming sorrow and sighing to joy. The totality of all the forefathers' service was to reveal and make known His G-dliness in the world — the sole purpose of man's existence, and all his joy and vitality. For it is known to all that this world is full of anger, anguish, sorrow, and sighing, ending in death, and the days of our lives are vanity, etc. There is no joy or vitality in the world except through the sparkling of truth and <span class="h">emunah</span>. <span class="mx">"Truth is the elixir of life,"</span> as our Sages say. The essential truth is Hashem: <span class="v">"the truth of Hashem is forever."</span> To believe in the truth of His existence and to strive and labor all one's days to draw close and cling to Him: <span class="v">"to Him you shall cling"</span> — this is all vitality: <span class="v">"choose life, so that you and your seed may live — to love Hashem your G-d and to cling to Him, for He is your life."</span></div>

<div class="p">But this clinging in completeness is impossible unless one shatters all bodily desires completely — like the forefathers, who merited drawing holy <span class="h">emunah</span> upon themselves each time until they came to great joy through this. Through this they sorted each time from the Chambers of Exchange, ascending through all the above ascents, perceiving the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light in <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span> — each according to his level, for there are many levels in this. The entire order of every Jew's daily service — especially the order of <span class="h">tefilah</span> with <span class="h">tallis</span> and <span class="h">tefillin</span>, etc. — is all the aspect of the above ascents explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Amtza'isa</span>": raising <span class="h">Malchus</span> from the <span class="h">klipos</span>, <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span> clothing <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span>, and <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span> clothing <span class="h">Chochmah-Binah-Da'as</span>, etc. — from world to world; see the <span class="h">kavanos</span> and the above <span class="h">Torah</span> well, and a little will sparkle for you.</div>

<div class="p">Although the forefathers and great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> shattered the body completely, meriting the essential completeness of this joy in Him (<span class="v">"rejoice, <i>tzadikim</i>, in Hashem"</span>; <span class="v">"the righteous shall rejoice and exult before G-d and be glad with joy"</span>) — their entire labor was to rectify all the world, to draw this holy joy and vitality into the heart of every Jew. Even one who has not completely shattered his bodily desires can also draw close to Hashem through guarding the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. Even one who has flawed as he has flawed — whenever he desires and strives to draw close to Hashem, he can gladden and revive his soul through the great power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>: by believing that every single good point merited through every <span class="h">mitzvah</span> he performs is beyond measure. Even the good points of the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> performed by the sinners of Israel surpass all the pleasures, wealth, etc. of the entire world — until they greatly gladden their souls that they too merit such true, eternal good: engaging in <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, through which they cling to Hashem, the Life of all lives, the Source of the joy of all joys. As it says: <span class="v">"I shall come to the G-d of my joyful exultation"</span>; <span class="v">"You shall make known to me the path of life; fullness of joys is Your countenance"</span> — all the aspect of <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain"</span>: seizing sorrow and sighing and transforming it to joy.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of all the exiles Israel undergoes — whose essence is the exile of the soul, far from its Father in Heaven, whose essential exile is sadness, as above. Due to the sin of Adam HaRishon and the corruptions of each generation, one must be in exile — until, through the power of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, the sorting from the Chambers of Exchange is accomplished specifically through this. The essential sorting is through joy — transforming sorrow and sighing to joy. This is the aspect of Yosef's sale to Egypt, through which specifically they could survive in Egypt: <span class="v">"G-d sent me before you for sustenance."</span> Yosef is the inflaming of joy through the above aspect, as above. Even Yaakov our father and his holy sons — the totality of Israel's holiness — were compelled to descend to Egypt, and specifically there Yaakov lived in joy and contentment. For then he perceived in completeness that the final <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> would come specifically through this: the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> descending into the very depths of the Chambers of Exchange (= Egypt and all the exiles named after Egypt, as is known), sorting the holiness from there specifically — all through transforming sorrow and sighing to joy: the essential sorting from the Chambers of Exchange. The Chambers of Exchange want the opposite — to exchange and reverse everything, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — their reversed reasoning beyond telling, without end. But the single point of the great true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> surpasses everything — they exchange and reverse for good, transforming sorrow and sighing to joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 52 — Yaakov's Burial in <i>Eretz Yisrael</i>: The Lights of the <i>Tzachtzachos</i> and the Resurrection</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yaakov made Yosef swear to bury him in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. There is the essential holy Place deriving from the above supernal Place (= the Arranger, called Place), from which the above nine chambers are perceived — the lights of the <span class="h">tzachtzachos</span> [brilliant splendors]. This is the essential perception of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, merited in completeness after their passing: <span class="v">"He shall satisfy your soul with <i>tzachtzachos</i>"</span> etc. Death was decreed because Adam HaRishon flawed the Arranger and Settler, as above — for which death was decreed for all generations. Now it is impossible for anyone, even the most complete <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, to attain the lights of the <span class="h">tzachtzachos</span> in their lifetime in completeness; each according to his service perceives what he perceives in this lifetime. Ultimately one must die and return to the dust: <span class="v">"to dust you shall return."</span></div>

<div class="p">There specifically, the body will decompose, be purified, and be transformed to holy dust — the aspect of holy ground (= the above awesome Place). This is the transformation from sadness, sorrow, and sighing to vitality and joy — through which one will rise at the Resurrection of the Dead, when the essential joy will be in completeness: <span class="v">"then our mouths will be filled with laughter"</span>; <span class="mx">"in the future, Hashem will make a dance for the righteous, each one pointing with his finger"</span> etc. — <span class="v">"we shall exult and rejoice in His salvation."</span> Fortunate is one who merits burial in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, where the holy Place resides — through which one is encompassed in the above Place (<span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place"</span>), perceiving the lights of the <span class="h">tzachtzachos</span> (= the nine chambers). Fortunate is one who merits this.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 53 — Yaakov Sought to Reveal the End — and Was Obstructed</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yaakov called to his sons"</span> etc. — he sought to reveal the end of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>, but the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> departed from him. The essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> is through joy: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out."</span> At first Yaakov thought the sorting from the Chambers of Exchange was already complete enough to reveal the end. But the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> departed — the aspect of <span class="mx">"into what is wondrous for you, do not seek"</span>: from the obstructer, which strikes the pursuing mind and does not allow it to pursue excessively, especially regarding the end of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> — the wondrous end (<span class="mx">"the heart does not reveal to the mouth"</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore he began speaking of other matters: <span class="v">"gather together"</span> etc. — the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> through the gathering of Israel, the aspect of peace: gathering and listening to Yaakov and Yisrael (= the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>). Through this, the complete <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> will ultimately come through their awesome power — through transforming sorrow and sighing to joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 54 — Yaakov's Rebuke of the Three Tribes: The Flaw of the Arranger and Settler</div>

<div class="p">Yaakov rebuked the first three tribes — his essential rebuke was that they flawed the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger and Settler, from which the errors and mistakes of even great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> derive, as above.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Reuven, you are my firstborn, my strength, the beginning of my vigor"</span> — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the <span class="h">bris</span>, as our Sages expound. There is the essential joy: <span class="v">"strength and gladness are in His place"</span> — the opposite of the flaw of the <span class="h">bris</span> (= wailing and sadness). Since he was born in such holiness, he deserved <span class="v">"extra dignity and extra strength"</span> (= priesthood and kingship, the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>). Through joy <span class="h">Malchus</span> is raised — the aspect of <span class="h">oz</span> [strength]: <span class="v">"Hashem — in Your strength the king rejoices"</span>; and all the above blessings — <span class="v">"extra dignity"</span>: <span class="v">"Aharon raised his hands to the people and blessed them."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"But reckless as water — you shall not excel"</span>: he rushed to show his anger — the above flaw. His mind pursued excessively until he came to anger — seeking to overturn the holy knowledge of Yaakov our father who placed his bed in Bilhah's tent. This is the flaw of the mind pursuing excessively through the flaw of the Arranger and Settler. <span class="v">"For then you desecrated my couch; he went up"</span> — the perception of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light that Yaakov merited on his couch in awesome holiness, through properly rectifying the Arranger and Settler, through sorting joy from the Chambers of Exchange (the secret of why he had to raise holy tribes also from the handmaids). The essential above perceptions are merited by the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> through the holiness of their union in ultimate holiness (as explained in Hilchos Periyah v'Riviyah 4 and in this <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Amtza'isa</span>"; see there well). Reuven flawed greatly in this — seeking to outthink Yaakov's <span class="h">da'as</span>, thereby flawing the Arranger and Settler — and therefore lost the priesthood and kingship (= the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>, through which the Arranger is essentially rectified).</div>

<div class="p">Likewise: <span class="v">"Shimon and Levi are brothers; instruments of violence are their wares."</span> When one tries to seize knowledge and perceptions one is unworthy of — this is the aspect of theft, robbery, and anger. <span class="v">"Let my soul not enter their counsel"</span> — <span class="v">"their secret [<i>sodam</i>]"</span> specifically: it seemed to them they had already attained all the secrets and perceptions, until they insisted on selling Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span> — seeming to them, according to their knowledge, that he strayed from the path of truth. Yaakov said: <span class="v">"let my soul not enter their counsel"</span> — I do not want such secrets. <span class="v">"And in their assembly — of Korach and Zimri — let my honor not be joined"</span> — they flawed even more greatly, drawn after their desires. Zimri wanted to commit adultery and argued with Moshe like a supposed scholar: "if you say this one is forbidden..." etc. Any person of true understanding knows the difference between Moshe Rabbainu's intent and Zimri's — Moshe was a great ascetic who married Yisro's daughter by Divine command, while Zimri followed his desire and brazenly wanted to permit public immorality. Korach desired personal honor, posing as an advocate for Israel: <span class="v">"the entire congregation is holy, and Hashem is in their midst — why do you exalt yourselves?"</span></div>

<div class="p">All this derives from the flaw of the Arranger and Settler of the mind. Even great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> like Shimon and Levi flawed in this subtly — not from desire, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, but from excessive inflaming for Hashem (= the flaw of the Arranger and Settler) — therefore they erred regarding Yosef. But Zimri and Korach flawed in this grossly — flawing the Settler until they lost all shame, brazening their faces as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yaakov pleaded: <span class="v">"in their assembly let my honor not be joined"</span> — <span class="v">"my honor"</span> specifically. Yaakov is the root of Israel's holiness — their essential honor of Hashem through the service of <span class="h">tefilah</span> with songs and praises, raising His honor through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, meriting the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the pursuit and obstructer properly: <span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place,"</span> as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span> — the ultimate completeness of unification. All unifications derive from there: when the pursuit is encompassed in the obstructer in <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span> properly, thereby begetting perceptions of G-dliness properly. Therefore Yaakov said: <span class="v">"let my honor not be joined"</span> specifically — through their assembly, my honor will not be unified where it must be unified (<span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place"</span>). This unification is accomplished only through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and through the holy gathering attached to them who nullify their minds before them — not through those who dispute Moshe and Aharon (= the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>). Korach and Zimri greatly flawed the Arranger and Settler, from which all holy unifications derive.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yehudah — your brothers shall thank you"</span> — from him emerged David and <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who will merit this in completeness, rectifying everything speedily in our days, <span class="h">amain</span>. David made himself all his days like a poor and destitute man: <span class="v">"I am a worm and not a man"</span>; <span class="v">"Hashem, my heart is not haughty, my eyes not lofty; I did not walk in great matters and wonders beyond me"</span> — concluding: <span class="v">"let Israel hope in Hashem from now and forever."</span> The essential thing: to make oneself like a nursing child, etc. — and hope for Hashem's salvation, increasing songs, praises, prayers, and thanksgivings. Like David, who engaged in this all his days until he merited composing <span class="h">Sefer Tehillim</span>, encompassing all ten types of melody (= the totality of joy in Him). This is the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> through which the complete <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> will come — through <span class="h">Mashiach</span> from his seed, whose <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> is through joy. Therefore he labored greatly to find the place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where the light of holy joy is received: <span class="v">"I shall rejoice in Hashem"</span>; <span class="v">"in Him our hearts rejoice."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 55 — The Twelve Tribes = The Twelve Months: Repairing the Moon's Blemish</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the twelve holy tribes Yaakov begot — the totality of Israel's holiness, the totality of joy in Him. They are twelve corresponding to the twelve months, as is known — for the twelve months are the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the moon's blemish, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="v">"luminaries"</span> [spelled deficiently]. This is accomplished through the holy joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 56 — The Birth of Each Tribe = A <i>Tikun</i> of Holy Joy</div>

<div class="p">Each tribe's birth was in awesome holiness — the aspect of holy joy, the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the <span class="h">bris</span>, the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the holy union (as explained in Hilchos Periyah v'Riviyah). Therefore all their holy names were called after the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> accomplished through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Reuven</span> — named for <span class="mx">"see the difference between my son and my father-in-law's son"</span> (= Esav, from whom all exiles come through sadness: <span class="v">"when you shall have cause to grieve,"</span> as above, ois yud-ches). Esav sold the birthright to Yaakov (= the holy joy of the sacrificial service — he had no desire for blessing, and it was distant from him). Afterward he grew jealous and pursued — the aspect of all exiles. Reuven is the opposite: he did not sell the birthright to Yosef; nevertheless he saved him — yielding his honor and nullifying himself before Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (= the essential joy of Israel). Therefore Reuven is the aspect of sorting from the Chambers of Exchange: <span class="mx">"see the difference between my son and my father-in-law's son"</span> — Reuven's holiness (the firstborn of Israel's seed) was sorted from the <span class="h">klipah</span> of Esav, through the joy with which he was born from Yaakov in such holiness (for he was the beginning of his vigor — the essential holiness of begetting is through joy). Therefore Reuven was the first to begin <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, as the Midrash states (Beraishis Rabbah 84) — from him Hoshayah the prophet emerged. The essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is sorting from the Chambers of Exchange, transforming sins to merits — transforming sorrow and sighing to joy (as explained in Hilchos Periyah v'Riviyah).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Shimon</span> — the aspect of the worthy rebuker: <span class="v">"Hashem heard that I am hated."</span> The rebuker is hated (as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">V'Aileh HaMishpatim</span>," <span class="lr">LM I:10</span>). This is the aspect of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> going forth to arouse all to Hashem — the worthy rebuker who arouses the whole world to Hashem.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Levi</span> — from him came the <span class="h">Kohanim</span>, with whom all blessings reside: <span class="v">"Aharon raised his hands to the people and blessed them"</span> — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the hands, <span class="h">Chesed-Gevurah-Tiferes</span>, as explained there.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Yehudah</span> — <span class="v">"this time I shall thank Hashem"</span>: the ultimate <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving, the essential completeness of joy through which one ascends to the Arranger. Then <span class="v">"she stopped bearing"</span> — one must wait, as above. Even though more mental faculties (= holy offspring) must still be begotten, one cannot pursue excessively.</div>

<div class="p">Then Rachel grew jealous of her sister: <span class="v">"give me children; if not, I am dead."</span> She saw and understood that more holy offspring of Israel's seed were still needed (= the totality of joy in Him). She said: <span class="v">"if not, I am dead"</span> — without children (= the essential joy), she is surely dead, for sadness is the side of death. <span class="v">"Yaakov's anger burned against Rachel: 'Am I in place of G-d?'"</span> — it is impossible to force the time. She said: <span class="v">"here is my handmaid Bilhah"</span> — she understood through <span class="h">ruach hakodesh</span> that it was impossible to beget further holy offspring unless he descended again to the Chambers of Exchange and sorted the holiness from there. This parallels what Sarah our mother understood — Avraham could not beget Yitzchak without first marrying the handmaid Hagar. But from that union emerged Yishmael, from whom exile (= sadness) comes: <span class="mx">"a foolish son is his mother's grief"</span> — his mother was expelled because of him (Midrash). But Yaakov's bed was complete: he merited descending and ascending in ultimate completeness, raising holy tribes even from the handmaids (= joy) — sorting in completeness from the Chambers of Exchange that cling to the handmaids.</div>

<div class="p">When Bilhah her handmaid bore a son, she named him <span class="h">Dan</span>: <span class="v">"G-d has judged me and also heard my voice."</span> She boasted that a holy son was sorted from the severity of judgment — sadness (= the severity of judgment) transformed to such joy that a holy tribe was born. Each of the tribes is the aspect of <span class="v">"a wise son gladdens a father."</span> This was through her many prayers (<span class="v">"also heard my voice"</span>). Through this, <span class="h">Malchus</span> was sorted from the <span class="h">klipos</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">Dan</span>: <span class="mx">"the law of the kingdom is law,"</span> as explained elsewhere.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Naftali</span> — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the feet: <span class="v">"Naftali is a hind let loose"</span>; <span class="v">"in the valley, he sent forth his feet"</span> (Shoftim 5).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Gad</span> — the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the hands: <span class="v">"he tears the arm and also the skull"</span> of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>. Through him the hands were rectified — through which the blessing of intellect is received (= the head and skull): <span class="v">"raise your hands in holiness"</span>; <span class="v">"Aharon raised his hands"</span> — they must be raised to the head to draw the blessing of intellect. Through this he tears the arm and skull of the disputants and idolaters. Leah said: <span class="h">"bagad"</span> [dealt treacherously] — Rashi: <span class="mx">"when he came to my handmaid, it was like a man being unfaithful to the wife of his youth"</span> — he descended to the Chambers of Exchange (= the handmaids). But Leah boasted in this, for through this comes the essential <span class="h">tikun</span>. <span class="h">"Ba Gad"</span> — good fortune has come! The essential blessing of <span class="h">mazal tov</span> recited with joy at a birth is specifically through this — through sorting from the Chambers of Exchange.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Asher</span> — <span class="v">"in my good fortune, for women have called me fortunate"</span>: the completeness of joy — <span class="v">"fortunate is the man"</span> — with which David began <span class="h">Sefer Tehillim</span>. The aspect of <span class="mx">"fortunate are we — how good is our portion!"</span> with which we boast daily. <span class="v">"Fortunate are you, Israel"</span> — with which Moshe concluded his blessings for Asher (in <span class="h">Parshas V'Zos HaBerachah</span>); all Moshe's blessings parallel Yaakov our father's blessings, as Rashi explains.</div>

<div class="p">Then Reuven found the <span class="h">duda'im</span> [mandrakes] in the field — the aspect of <span class="v">"the mandrakes give forth fragrance."</span> Both mandrakes — the good and the bad — both gave forth fragrance, as our Sages say. This is the great power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who gladden all and transform sorrow and sighing to joy, through which comes the essential begetting. Rachel wanted them, and Leah said: <span class="v">"is it a small thing that you took my husband?"</span> — she saw through <span class="h">ruach hakodesh</span> that she still needed to beget holy offspring. Then <span class="h">Yissachar</span> was born — the great Torah scholars, the essential joy: <span class="v">"the statutes of Hashem are upright, gladdening the heart"</span> — the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>. From the sons of Yissachar came those who understood the times — to intercalate years and fix the months: the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the moon's blemish through joy. <span class="v">"To know what Israel should do"</span> — they knew how to gladden each person, teaching him what to do at each time to be saved from the bitterness of exile (= sadness), and to merit the joy of Israel — the totality of holiness.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Zevulun</span> — <span class="v">"rejoice, Zevulun, in your going out."</span> Zevulun engaged in commerce to support his brother Yissachar the Torah scholar — the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of wealth and money, where the essential hold of sadness resides (<span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span>). The <span class="h">tikun</span> is through Zevulun supporting Yissachar: <span class="v">"rejoice, Zevulun."</span></div>

<div class="p">Then: <span class="v">"G-d remembered Rachel"</span> etc. Even though ten holy tribes had already been born who reveal His G-dliness to gladden in Him always — nevertheless, Yosef still needed to be born, through whom comes the essential inflaming of Israel's joy. He is <span class="v">"a man in whom the spirit of G-d resides"</span> — fanning the dust from the Jewish heart each time. Therefore Hashem's mercy was aroused, and He remembered Rachel on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span> was born, through whom comes the completeness of joy.</div>

<div class="p">Then immediately Yaakov said: <span class="v">"send me away"</span> — so the bed would not be completed outside <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, as above. He merited that Binyamin was born in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> — the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> in his portion. After the joy was completed through the birth of the tribes, the Place was still needed — to receive the light of this joy: the awesome Place of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, the source of joy: <span class="v">"eternal joy upon their heads."</span></div>

<div class="p">Rachel was remembered on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> like Sarah our mother (who bore Yitzchak on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>). On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> the world was created — to reveal His G-dliness in the world, all the joy, the vitality and sustenance of the world: <span class="v">"Hashem shall rejoice in His works."</span> Sarah was remembered and bore Yitzchak — the first holy birth, named for joy. But the essential begetting of the complete bed (= all Israel) was through Yaakov, who begot twelve tribes — the totality of joy: <span class="v">"let Israel rejoice in its Maker."</span> The bed's completion was through Rachel, who bore Yosef and Binyamin — therefore she too was remembered on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, when the Creation must be rectified and Adam HaRishon's sin (<span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span> — from which all sadness and exiles come) must be repaired. On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> we engage in this <span class="h">tikun</span>, reading the portion of Hashem remembering Sarah; and Rachel's remembrance (for through her bearing Yosef and Binyamin comes the completeness of joy that began with Yitzchak). On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> we blow the <span class="h">shofar</span> — encompassing all ten types of melody (= the totality of joy): <span class="v">"fortunate is the people that knows the <i>teruah</i>"</span>; <span class="v">"in Your Name they shall exult all day"</span>; <span class="v">"the joy of Hashem is your strength,"</span> said regarding <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>. Chanah too was remembered on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, bearing Shmuel the prophet — who anointed Shaul and David: the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Malchus</span>, from which <span class="h">Mashiach</span> will emerge to redeem Israel, when all joy will be complete, speedily in our days, <span class="h">amain</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Menasheh</span> and <span class="h">Efrayim</span>. <span class="h">Menasheh</span> — <span class="v">"G-d has made me forget all my toil"</span>: the toil, sorrow, etc. <span class="h">Efrayim</span> — <span class="v">"G-d has made me fruitful in the land of my affliction"</span>: not only was he saved from the slavery (= sadness, toil, and sorrow), and in His kindness <span class="v">"made me forget all my toil"</span> — He even <span class="v">"made me fruitful in the land of my affliction"</span>: the transformation of poverty and sadness to joy.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 57 — "These Are the Names of the Children of Israel Coming to Egypt": Survival Through Joy</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"These are the names of the children of Israel coming to Egypt, with Yaakov"</span> etc. When the Torah begins telling of the Egyptian exile and redemption, it starts with the names of the children of Israel who came there — to show that all their survival in exile and their redemption derived from the power of the names of the children of Israel: Reuven, Shimon, Levi, etc. — all named for the joy of Israel. Through this they sorted from the Chambers of Exchange, perceiving the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light — the essential purpose of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>: <span class="v">"you shall know that I am Hashem who brings you out."</span></div>

<div class="p">Hashem arranged that Yaakov and his righteous sons would descend to Egypt first, for through their awesome holiness (the complete bed revealing His G-dliness in the world = the essential joy and vitality), they had the strength to bear the exile's bitterness without being swallowed up there. The essential bitterness of exile is sadness. The more exile is intensified, the more sadness overpowers — and the more sadness overpowers (= the exile of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span>), the more physical exile and slavery intensify. From where then shall our help come? All survival in exile and hope of leaving comes through the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — Yaakov and his sons who descended to Egypt — the above-named children of Israel. Through their great power and deep counsel, they can draw His G-dliness, reviving and gladdening every Jewish soul, raising <span class="h">Malchus</span> each time through all the ascents until the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Yosef was in Egypt"</span> — the essential survival and hope is through Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, <span class="v">"a man in whom there is spirit"</span> — who knows each time to fan the dust (= sadness) from the Jewish heart and inflame it for Hashem in gradation and measure. He knows how to transform all sorrow and sighing to gladness and joy. <span class="v">"All who emerged from Yaakov's thigh were seventy souls"</span> — the seventy faces of the Torah, the seventy shining countenances (= joy): <span class="v">"those who seek Your face — Yaakov"</span> etc. (as explained in the <span class="h">Torah</span> "<span class="h">Tzavisa Tzedek</span>"). <span class="v">"Yosef was in Egypt"</span> — the essential thing through Yosef, whom Hashem sent ahead as a remedy before the affliction. He was sold as a slave (= the bitterness of sadness = slavery), yet he stood the test and strengthened himself even there with joy, as it says: <span class="v">"Yosef was a successful man"</span> — the Midrash explains: leaping and dancing. In the bitterness of exile when sadness intensifies, the essential counsel is to do foolish things to gladden one's soul in such bitterness.</div>

<div class="p">As it says of David: he changed his behavior before Avimelech — being in great danger in a great exile, he did seemingly foolish acts: <span class="v">"he scribbled on the doors of the gate and let his saliva run down his beard"</span> etc. Therefore, their essential survival in Egypt and their exodus was through <span class="v">"Yosef was in Egypt"</span> — Hashem sent the <span class="h">tzadik</span> ahead, as above.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"They embittered their lives with hard labor, with mortar and bricks"</span> etc. The essential bitterness of exile is slavery — drawn from the contamination of the serpent: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span> — the overpowering of sadness. This is the miry clay David cried about: <span class="v">"I sank in deep mire, with no foothold... save me from the mire."</span> The holy Zohar explains: <span class="v">"with hard labor"</span> = with difficult questions; <span class="v">"with mortar"</span> = with the severity of <span class="h">Talmudic</span> argumentation; <span class="v">"and with bricks"</span> = with the clarification of <span class="h">halachah</span>. The essential bitterness of exile confuses the joy in Him (= holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, shining countenances) through the difficulties and confusions — the severity of argumentation and clarification of <span class="h">halachah</span>. The essential "raising a teaching to the level of <span class="h">halachah</span>" is to clarify and whiten true counsel for serving the Creator — to leave the contamination of this lowly world and draw close to Hashem, for <span class="mx">"not study but practice is the essential thing."</span> This is why the essential drawing close to Torah scholars and true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> is so important — so they can teach us the way to walk and the deed to do at each time, so each person merits leaving what he must leave and returning to Hashem.</div>

<div class="p">But the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> — the bitterness of exile (= sadness) — overpowers greatly, embittering Israel's lives with difficulties and confusions of <span class="h">emunah</span> and confusions of counsel, until people do not know how to whiten the <span class="h">halachah</span> — the path and way to return to Hashem. <span class="v">"With all labor in the field"</span> — the craving for money and the burden of livelihood, compelled to leave the tent of Torah and go to the field to engage in livelihood, until one is so burdened with all field-labor and enticed away from even setting fixed times for Torah study.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"All their labor was with <i>parech</i>"</span> — our Sages expound: <span class="h">b'feh rach</span> [with a soft mouth]. At first the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> and <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> enter with a soft mouth, clothing themselves in <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, saying: "It's impossible to sit in the <span class="h">bais midrash</span> all day; you must go to the field to work and do business. Do you want to die or be a pauper?" etc. — until they cast him into the deep miry clay: the dark countenances of sadness over money (<span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span>). Even when he earns, it never suffices — he always craves more: <span class="mx">"the more possessions, the more worry"</span> — <span class="v">"it was not known they had come."</span> They entice him to build Pisom and Ramses — great, tall buildings for themselves: <span class="v">"they call their houses by their names forever"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 58 — Pharaoh's Decree: "Every Son Born — Cast into the River"</div>

<div class="p">This is Pharaoh's decree: <span class="v">"every son that is born..."</span> etc.; <span class="v">"if it is a son, kill him; if a daughter, she shall live."</span> A son — the male side (= joy): <span class="mx">"a wise son gladdens a father."</span> A daughter — the female side, where the essential hold of sadness resides: <span class="v">"her feet descend to death."</span> Pharaoh wanted to strengthen the female side (= sadness) — the essential force of exile.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"The midwives feared G-d and did not do as Pharaoh commanded; they kept the children alive."</span> The midwives are the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of the generation — who give birth, raising worthy students in Israel (considered as if they birthed them, as our Sages say). They acted opposite to the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> (= Pharaoh and Egypt, whose entire agenda is to strengthen sadness through many stratagems — primarily through the burden of money and livelihood, and sometimes through the reversed reasoning from the Chambers of Exchange that cast a person down through improper rebuke, etc.). But the above midwives (= the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>) do the opposite: not only do they not cast people down with their words as the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> desires, but <span class="v">"they kept the children alive"</span> — reviving their students and those attached to them through their true words, restoring their souls through comforting counsel, gladdening them through their power and deep counsel, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the midwives were named <span class="h">Shifrah</span> and <span class="h">Pu'ah</span>: <span class="h">Shifrah</span> — she beautifies the infant; <span class="h">Pu'ah</span> — she cries out to the infant, etc., as our Sages say. This is how the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> conduct themselves with the Jewish souls attached to them: they beautify them and make them lovely through all kinds of counsel to revive and strengthen them in every good point found in them, etc. And they cry out to them like to an actual infant — whistling and calling to them not to let themselves fall in any way, but to trust in Hashem's kindnesses that are never exhausted, and in the great power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who will never abandon them: <span class="v">"not according to our sins does He deal with us"</span>; there is always hope (as explained extensively in the holy <span class="h">sefarim</span>, especially in our writings; see there well).</div>

<div class="sn">§ 59 — The Burning Bush: The Fire of Joy Amidst the Thorns of Exile</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the vision of the <span class="h">Sneh</span> [bush] and why Moshe refused his mission for so long. Essentially, he saw through <span class="h">ruach hakodesh</span> the length of the bitter present exile — impossible to fathom its end, for it is the wondrous end. Therefore he did not want to go on his mission, as our Sages say (explained in Rashi on <span class="v">"send, please, by the hand You will send"</span>): "I know I will not bring them into <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and will not redeem them in the future," etc. — all because of the great dispute among Israel, until they do not know where the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are — the essential prolonging of exile, as is known.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"He saw, and behold, the bush burned with fire, yet the bush was not consumed."</span> He was greatly astonished: why is the bush not consumed? The bush alludes to the exile, as our Sages say. He saw the revelation of G-dliness in a flame of fire from within the bush — the inflaming of the Jewish heart burning for Hashem through the power of the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (<span class="v">"a man in whom there is spirit"</span>) — burning for Hashem from within the bush, from within the bitterness of exile. Yet <span class="v">"the bush was not consumed"</span> — the exile still overpowers, the bitterness of sadness (<span class="v">"the bush was not consumed"</span>: the thorns and briers surrounding the rose, whose essence is sadness = the exile of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span>) is not consumed, overpowering each time.</div>

<div class="p">Moshe was greatly astonished and said: <span class="v">"let me turn aside and see this great sight — why the bush is not consumed."</span> He wanted to reach the very end — how Israel will merit the complete <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> with no exile after it, consuming the bush (= the thorns and briers) completely. Then: <span class="v">"Hashem saw that he turned aside to look, and called to him... 'Do not come closer... remove your shoes from your feet, for the place upon which you stand is holy ground.'"</span> This is the obstructer — the Arranger and Settler who prevents the pursuing mind from breaking through to Hashem, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Moshe, the first righteous redeemer, saw he would redeem Israel — he could gladden Israel amidst the exile's bitterness, illuminating within them the sparkling of His G-dliness (= the essential joy): a flame of fire, <span class="v">"its sparks are sparks of fire, a flame of G-d"</span> — revealed from within the bush, from within the exile's bitterness, for he could transform sorrow and sighing to joy. Yet <span class="v">"the bush was not consumed"</span> — they overpower each time with many stratagems without limit. They caused Israel to sin greatly immediately after the giving of the Torah, etc. — the ten trials, etc. — through which all the exiles were decreed until this long, bitter exile. As it says: <span class="v">"on the day I make account..."</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">Moshe wanted to fathom this — the perception of the wondrous end. Hashem said: <span class="v">"do not come closer"</span> — the obstructer, the Arranger and Settler. For Moshe then merited the perception of the above nine chambers through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> (= the sparkling of receiving the Torah that began then), perceiving and seeing what he perceived. But he saw the bush was still not consumed and said: <span class="v">"let me turn aside and see"</span> — how to perceive G-dliness with such joy as to consume the bush completely. As explained above: one depends on the other — the more one overpowers with the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, the more one merits some sprouting of salvation, raising the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> from exile and ascending through all the above ascents until the nine chambers where the source of joy resides (<span class="v">"eternal joy upon their heads"</span>). Then one draws additional joy from the source, and then one can sort even more from the Chambers of Exchange — and then ascend even higher to the nine chambers in greater perception, and joy grows even more, and one can sort even more — and so on. If this had proceeded in order, certainly the end of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> (through joy) would have arrived. But our sins have caused this — each time the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> overpowers, causing Israel to sin and strengthening sadness: <span class="v">"they provoked Him in the wilderness"</span> — confusions of <span class="h">emunah</span> (= idolatry and heresy, called <span class="h">atzabim</span> [idols] for sadness [<span class="h">atzvus</span>]: <span class="v">"their idols are silver and gold"</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Through this the exile is prolonged — <span class="mx">"Israel was exiled only because faithful people ceased among them"</span> etc. (= sadness itself). But the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of every generation labor with their great power to gladden Israel through illuminating holy <span class="h">emunah</span> in their hearts each time. But the essential prolonging of exile is through the dispute that strives to distance Jewish hearts from the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. Through this, the ultimate end cannot be fathomed — even Moshe Rabbainu could not, for Hashem told him: <span class="v">"do not come closer"</span> — the Chambers of Exchange have not yet been sufficiently sorted for you to ascend and perceive further. All perception of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> light depends on the sorting from the Chambers of Exchange through <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Remove your shoes from your feet, for the place upon which you stand is holy ground"</span> — to here extends the holiness of the above supernal Place (= the obstructer, <span class="v">"blessed is the glory of Hashem from His place"</span>). One needs the removal of shoes — stripping oneself completely from the body of this world, as stated in the holy Zohar. But to go further — to perceive how to consume the bush completely — is impossible now. This too is the aspect of the waiting: one must wait a great deal until we merit such sorting from the Chambers of Exchange that the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> will perceive how to consume the bush completely.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Moshe indeed refused his mission for seven days, saying: <span class="v">"send by the hand You will send,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 60 — Aharon's Joy: "He Saw You and Rejoiced in His Heart" — The Root of the <i>Ge'ulah</i></div>

<div class="p">Until finally: <span class="v">"Hashem's anger burned against Moshe, and He said: 'Is not Aharon your brother the Levi?... He is coming out to meet you, and when he sees you, he will rejoice in his heart.'"</span> <span class="v">"He will rejoice in his heart"</span> specifically — through this comes the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>: through the great peace he would have with Moshe, not being jealous at all. On the contrary — rejoicing in his heart. The essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is through joy — raising <span class="h">Malchus</span> from the Chambers of Exchange, etc. — meriting the above blessings: the holiness of the priesthood (<span class="v">"Aharon raised his hands and blessed"</span>). Therefore Aharon merited the holiness of the priesthood through <span class="v">"he will rejoice in his heart,"</span> as our Sages say.</div>

<div class="p">Through this came the Egyptian redemption — for the essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> is through joy, and the essential completeness of joy is through peace: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out and in peace be led."</span> Hashem revealed to Moshe that he must go on his mission to redeem Israel and give us the Torah — without him, Israel would never be redeemed, as the Midrash states: <span class="mx">"if you do not redeem them, no one else will."</span> Specifically because of the length of the present exile — he must go and give the Torah. Had we not received the Torah through Moshe Rabbainu, survival in this long, bitter exile would be impossible. As the holy Zohar states: our essential hope now is that Hashem already provided the remedy before the affliction — we already received the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> through Moshe. Through this we can gladden ourselves each time through the abundance of <span class="h">mitzvos</span> we merit performing daily (even the simplest person) — essentially through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> of each generation who instill in us the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>-points, etc. — raising the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> each time until the complete <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Without the Torah, it would be impossible to gladden our souls now or leave the exile. In Egypt, where the exile of Edom had not yet intensified, the holiness of Yaakov and his sons (especially Yosef the <span class="h">tzadik</span>) sufficed to sustain and survive the exile. But now, in the crushing pressure of the final exile, survival without the Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> (which are our life and joy: <span class="v">"we shall rejoice in the words of Your Torah and Your <i>mitzvos</i> forever, for they are our life"</span>; <span class="v">"were Your Torah not my delight, I would have perished in my affliction"</span>) would be impossible.</div>

<div class="p">But the essential power of the Egyptian <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> then was through <span class="v">"he saw you and rejoiced in his heart"</span> — Aharon, who merited the holiness of the priesthood through this (the blessing of the hands, etc. — through which the nine chambers are perceived, from which all perception of G-dliness derives — the essential purpose of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>). The essential delay of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> then was through Dasan and Aviram — the heads of the disputants and their followers, from whom all corruptions and trials came, leading to all the destructions (the first and second <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, all the sufferings, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, up to this final exile whose essential prolonging is through dispute, as above). In every dispute there are sparks of Dasan and Aviram, as our Sages say.</div>

<div class="p">Nevertheless: <span class="v">"You are eternally exalted, Hashem."</span> Then Aharon the <span class="h">Kohain</span> was found — not jealous of the greatness of Moshe his younger brother. On the contrary: he went out to meet him with joy — through which came the first <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>, and the holiness of the priesthood was drawn. Through the power of Moshe and Aharon the <span class="h">Kohain</span>, there are found now, in every generation, true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people who attach themselves to the great chosen <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, rejoicing to meet them, heeding their counsel, gladdening their souls in performing every <span class="h">mitzvah</span>. Through this will come the complete <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out and in peace be led,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 61 — <i>Birchas SheHechiyanu</i> [The Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive]: The Joy of Seeing a Beloved Fellow Jew</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Birchas SheHechiyanu</span> [the Blessing of Who Has Kept Us Alive]: when one sees a friend who is dear to him and rejoices at seeing him, he blesses <span class="h">SheHechiyanu</span> [Who Has Kept Us Alive]. This is the essential vitality and sustenance — when one rejoices at seeing a fellow Jew. The essential joy is that two Jews who love each other merited meeting together in peace — through which the joy of Israel is greatly increased. Every Jew has a portion of G-dliness from above — the source of joy: <span class="v">"let Israel rejoice in its Maker."</span> But the <span class="h">klipah</span> that precedes the fruit (= sadness) covers over it, and a great struggle is needed to overpower joy over sadness — a great battle. Therefore, many Jewish souls must join and gather together. The more souls join in love, the more joy increases — the portions of joy are gathered and joined, shining with even greater light: <span class="mx">"the light of the righteous makes glad"</span> (ois mem-bais above); <span class="mx">"when the righteous multiply, the people rejoice."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore, upon seeing a beloved fellow Jew, we bless: <span class="h">SheHechiyanu v'kiy'manu</span> [Who has kept us alive and sustained us] — for this is the essential vitality and sustenance [<span class="h">hechyah</span> and <span class="h">kiyum</span>], through joy and peace. This is <span class="v">"he saw you and rejoiced in his heart"</span> — through which came the essential Egyptian <span class="h">ge'ulah</span>: Aharon was not jealous of his younger brother Moshe's greatness. The essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> is through joy — essentially through the love of friends who strive to draw close to Hashem in truth: <span class="v">"he saw you and rejoiced in his heart."</span></div>

